The Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) is responsible for applying, administering and enforcing the anti-dumping and countervailing provisions of Canada’s Special Import Measures Act (SIMA). The CBSA conducts investigations to determine whether goods imported into Canada are dumped or subsidized, and whether these imports are injuring or threatening to injure Canadian producers. You may find more information on the CBSA’s role in investigations in the CBSA’s SIMA Handbook.

CBSA investigators do the following:

Conduct an investigation into the dumping or subsidizing of goods imported into Canada;

Determine whether the imports are causing injury or retardation, or are threatening to cause injury, to domestic producers of like goods; and

Determine whether the imports are the cause of the injury or retardation.

The CBSA makes its preliminary findings and recommendations public through the issuance of a CBSA Statement of Reasons. Canadian International Trade Tribunal

The Canadian International Trade Tribunal (CITT) is an independent administrative tribunal. The CITT reports to Parliament through the Minister of Finance. The CITT has a number of functions under the Special Import Measures Act (SIMA) and other legislation. The CITT’s role in anti-dumping and countervailing investigations is to determine whether the dumping or subsidizing of goods imported into Canada is causing injury or retardation, or is threatening to cause injury, to domestic producers of like goods. The CITT makes its decision public through a finding and a statement of reasons. If the CITT determines that the dumping or subsidizing of goods imported into Canada is causing injury or retardation, or is threatening to cause injury, to domestic producers of like goods, anti-dumping or countervailing duties are imposed on those imports. If the CITT determines that the dumping or subsidizing of goods imported into Canada is not causing injury or retardation, or is not threatening to cause injury, to domestic producers of like goods, no anti-dumping or countervailing duties are imposed. In addition, the CITT has other functions under SIMA, including:

Proceedings on the expiry of anti-dumping or countervailing duty orders;

Exclusions of goods from anti-dumping or countervailing duty orders;

Proceedings on the review of CBSA decisions to remit anti-dumping or countervailing duties;

Proceedings on the retroactive application of anti-dumping or countervailing duty orders;

Proceedings on the suspension of anti-dumping or countervailing duty orders; and

Proceedings on the review of CBSA decisions to grant or to refuse to grant an undertaking to exclude goods from anti-dumping or countervailing duty orders. In addition, the CITT has other functions under SIMA and other legislation, including:

Proceedings on complaints regarding the customs classification of goods imported into Canada; and

