No. You must not close the proposed transaction until you receive notice from the Bureau that you may do so or, if a request for additional information is issued, until you have provided all of the information requested. How will I know if my merger is reviewable and should be notified? You can consult this Merger Notification Guideline to determine whether or not you are required to notify the Bureau. In addition, the Bureau staff are available to discuss whether or not notification is required. If a notification is not provided when it is required, the Bureau can obtain a court order requiring notification and, in appropriate circumstances, can order parties to unwind an integration. I am considering purchasing shares of a public corporation. Do I need to notify? Generally, a purchase of voting shares of a public corporation would not constitute a merger where the total percentage of votes that are acquired in Canada is less than 20 percent, and those votes are not increased beyond that 20 percent as a result of the transaction (for example, a company which currently has 10 percent of the voting shares of a public corporation could not normally acquire an additional 10 percent without notifying). What are the basic steps in the merger notification process? The merging parties must file a detailed notification with the Commissioner and supply the Bureau with required information. The Bureau has up to 45 days to review the information. During this time, the Bureau may:
- request additional information from one or more of the merging parties;
- obtain an order from the Competition Tribunal to compel parties to provide information;
- determine that the merger is not likely to substantially prevent or lessen competition and end the review; or
- find that the merger is likely to substantially prevent or lessen competition and refer the merger to the Tribunal. The Bureau will not normally disclose information regarding the parties and the merger during the 45-day waiting period. What types of information are requested in a notification? The parties must provide the Bureau with certain information regarding the transaction and their operations. The Bureau will not normally request information regarding the purchaser where the vendor is disposing of its entire business in Canada or a substantial part of its business in Canada. The Bureau may request other information that is relevant to the review of the merger. The Bureau has issued Merger Notification Guidelines that set out the information that is normally requested by the Bureau. The Guidelines are available from the Competition Bureau or from the Bureau's website. Who pays for the cost of supplying information requested by the Bureau? Each party supplies the information that it has in its possession. Each party is responsible for its own out-of-pocket costs in preparing the information. The Bureau may, in appropriate circumstances, pay a party's costs of obtaining information, including the cost of retaining outside experts. What are the time limits in the merger notification process? There are no legislated time limits for the Bureau's review of a merger. In practice, the Bureau attempts to conduct its review within 120 days of receiving complete information. The Bureau will not normally extend the statutory waiting period of 45 days. If a merger is completed before the Bureau completes its review, the Bureau can obtain a court order to unscramble the merged firms. I have received a request for additional information from the Bureau. Is there any way that I can challenge the Bureau's request? The Bureau will normally not issue a request for additional information unless it is necessary to determine whether a merger is likely to substantially prevent or lessen competition. You can challenge the Bureau's request for additional information by applying to the Competition Tribunal for a stay of the request. A Tribunal stay will normally be granted only if you can show that the Bureau's request is unreasonable. If a stay is granted, the Bureau is prohibited from issuing further requests for information or otherwise proceeding with its review of the merger. A request for a stay can be filed at the same time that you respond to the Bureau's request for information. The Bureau will not normally disclose information regarding the parties and the merger during the 45-day waiting period. Are the Bureau's requests for information made public? No. The Bureau will not normally disclose information regarding the parties and the merger during the 45-day waiting period. Are the Bureau's requests for information made public? No. The Bureau will not normally disclose information regarding the parties and the merger during the 45-day waiting period. What is the Bureau's policy regarding information that parties claim is confidential? The Bureau recognizes that some of the information that it requests may be confidential. The Bureau attempts to treat all information as confidential. The Bureau will mark information as confidential if the parties request this and the Bureau agrees. In appropriate circumstances, the Bureau may require parties to submit information under seal. In other cases, the Bureau may require parties to submit a public version of sensitive information, with sensitive passages redacted. The Bureau will normally consult with merging parties prior to disclosing information to other government agencies and will normally obtain their consent. The Bureau will normally rely on the parties to identify information that is confidential. In appropriate

